and I designed many courses. Here I taught digital design here and in Concordia I taught

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:54.370
digital designs, digital design one and two, microprocessor systems,

00:01:55.090 --> 00:02:03.090
intro to computer organization and architecture, and also advanced computer architecture for

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:09.090
graduate students, network on chip, course in Concordia University, and here intro to

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:15.570
programming and problem solving, also known as CCS 174, and intro to engineering profession,

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:26.530
also known as EMGR 101. My style of teaching is very hands-on interactive and engaging and I see

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:34.450
this process as being partners in learning. I am going to be your guide and I hope that you will

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:42.210
reciprocate in participation and dedication because that is essential part for your learning

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:53.970
in your success. Overall, I do also research and service. For research, my most recent are

00:02:53.970 --> 00:03:00.850
IoT systems and engineering applications of artificial intelligence, machine learning,

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:08.530
deep linear learning, any of that is needed. I'm very passionate about inclusive computing

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:14.690
and computing and engineering education, and I do research in those fields. And I have

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:21.090
big experience in network relationship, parallel course, and also

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.290
computer architecture research, which is not listed here.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:33.010
Part of my service is mentoring, and I have been founding faculty advisor for

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:37.970
Student Organization of Women in Computing and recently I became faculty mentor for

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:43.330
Metric II program. Please google that and find it. I think it's a wonderful program. Maybe

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:52.850
some of you will be joining us. In addition to that, I do mentoring my research students

00:03:53.490 --> 00:04:01.890
and sometimes my former students and I find common topics to work on and we continue working

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:10.530
beyond our courses. Now let's switch gears to course delivery mode. This class is going to

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:16.530
be presented and taught in flip classroom model. Flip classroom model is the model in which

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:26.690
there is some assigned work at home. In our case it's going to be videos of theoretical concepts

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:31.690
that we need in order to practice in class.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:36.010
So you will be watching videos before each seminar class

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:40.010
and you're going to do a quiz before each seminar class.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.850
And in class, we are going to do activity.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:48.970
Motivation for this is that you are provided

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.330
with way more instructions.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:55.330
You are provided with more time to have Q&A

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:59.070
and you are provided with activities

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.770
in which you participate.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:05.270
There is opportunity for you to get more help.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:10.470
For you to get help, you only need to ask.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:12.490
And if you don't feel comfortable doing that

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:14.970
in front of entire class, please make an appointment

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.130
and see me during the office hours

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.890
or come to regular office hours without appointment.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:31.250
Other things that are super important for flip class is that that method of work keeps

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:35.870
students up to date because you have to prepare for every class.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:41.250
You don't end up cramming your studies before the exam.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:51.210
And also by the way that you participate with activities, the instructor, that is me, gets

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:54.790
the feedback and can adjust the class.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:06:03.370
Added value is that you get access to very different types of materials that suit for

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.650
different learners.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:10.890
So you're going to have videos, you're going to have quizzes, you're going to have problems

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.530
that you will be solving by yourself.

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:16.530
You're going to have, you know, xi books.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:19.170
So that is reading, and again, activities

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:22.050
and homework on top of it.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:25.650
And all the theory is going to be backed up

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:26.810
by lab activities.

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:30.090
So that's how the people who learn by doing

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:32.230
are going to benefit from this.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:37.850
I did research on flipped classroom on this class

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:44.410
in particular, but in general there is lots of research showing that this method actually

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:49.810
improves student engagement, that students as a result have a better academic performance

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:57.290
compared to a lecture-only style of learning, and they understand the concepts much deeper.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.890
So how are we going to do this?

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:04.890
In seminar, we are going to cover theory at home

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:09.210
by using videos.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.250
